export const llmsContent = "# txtfold\n\nCollapses repetition and surfaces outliers in large log files and structured data. Converts thousands of lines into a compact, LLM-readable summary. No ML — same input always produces the same output.\n\n## When to use\n\nUse txtfold before reading or passing a large file to an LLM:\n\n- Log files with thousands of repetitive lines\n- JSON arrays or maps with many similar records\n- Any structured data where you need patterns, anomalies, or a frequency breakdown\n- Before embedding or indexing records — remove duplicates first\n\nDo not use it on small files that already fit comfortably in context.\n\n## Recommended workflow\n\nWork iteratively. Do not guess at a pipeline for an unfamiliar file.\n\n**1. Learn the syntax** (once per session):\n```sh\ntxtfold --syntax\n```\n\n**2. Discover structure** — field paths, types, cardinality, sample values:\n```sh\ntxtfold --discover data.json\n```\nThe output includes a suggested pipeline selector. Use it to identify high-cardinality noise (unique IDs, blobs) and low-cardinality grouping candidates (status codes, categories).\n\n**3. Preview token cost** — before running analysis or handing output to an LLM:\n```sh\ntxtfold --cost-preview data.json\n```\nFields consuming >20% of the estimated token budget are flagged as noise candidates. Use `del()` to remove them. Aim for under ~2,000 tokens for comfortable LLM context.\n\n**4. Run initial analysis**:\n```sh\n# JSON\ntxtfold '.diagnostics[] | del(.sourceCode) | group_by(.category)' data.json\n\n# Plain log\ntxtfold 'patterns' app.log\n```\n\n**5. Iterate** — adjust based on results: add `top(N)` to focus, switch algorithms, refine filters, re-preview cost. Re-run until groups are distinct and output length is manageable.\n\n## Input formats\n\nFormat is inferred from file extension (`.json` → json, anything else → line). For stdin, `--format` is required.\n\n| Format  | Use for                             |\n| ------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `line`  | Plain text logs, CSV                |\n| `block` | Stack traces, multi-line log entries |\n| `json`  | JSON arrays or maps                 |\n\nFor `block` format, use `--entry-pattern <regex>` to mark entry boundaries, or rely on the timestamp heuristic.\n\n## Key flags\n\n| Flag                      | Effect                                              |\n| ------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--discover`              | Structural map of the file; no analysis             |\n| `--cost-preview`          | Token cost estimate per field; suggests `del()` calls |\n| `--output-format json`    | Machine-readable output (matches `output-schema.json`) |\n| `--budget <N>`            | Cap output at N lines                               |\n| `--depth <N>`             | Schema nesting depth for `schemas`/`subtree` (default 1) |\n| `--entry-pattern <regex>` | Entry boundary regex for `block` format             |\n| `--syntax`                | Print pipeline syntax reference and exit            |\n\n## Language bindings\n\n**Python**:\n```python\nimport txtfold\nresult = txtfold.process(data, input_format=\"json\", pipeline=\"del(.sourceCode) | schemas\")\n```\n\n**TypeScript/Node**:\n```ts\nimport { process } from \"txtfold\";\nconst result = process(data, { inputFormat: \"json\", pipeline: \"del(.sourceCode) | schemas\" });\n```";
